==============================================================================
RAPIDITY AS THE LANGUAGE OF SCALING LAWS
kind-pasteur-2026-03-15-S116e
==============================================================================

rapidity(x) = arctanh(x) for x in (-1,1)
rapidity(x) = ln(x)/2 for x > 0 (positive reals)
f(n) = (n+1)/n is the superparticular homomorphism
Q(x) = (1+x)/(1-x) maps addition to multiplication

==============================================================================
SECTION 1: ZIPF'S LAW IS LINEAR IN RAPIDITY
==============================================================================

Zipf's law: f(r) ~ C/r^s, typically s ~ 1.
Taking rapidity of both sides (as positive reals):
  rapidity(f(r)) = rapidity(C) - s * rapidity(r)
  ln(f(r))/2 = ln(C)/2 - s * ln(r)/2

This is a LINEAR relationship in rapidity space!
Slope = -s, intercept = rapidity(C).

